          {
            "type": "thinking",
            "thinking": "Yazaki and Sumitomo are both headquartered in Japan while Aptiv is US-based, but what matters for duty purposes isn't the company's home country — it's the actual country of origin of the goods being shipped.",
            "signature": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "**Roster countries:** Yazaki — JPN, Sumitomo Electric — JPN, Aptiv — USA.\n\n**On the duty question:** Yazaki and Sumitomo are the pair that match on the roster field. But I'd flag that this doesn't actually answer your question. US import duty is set by the *country of origin of the goods* — where the harness was substantially transformed — not by the supplier's nationality or headquarters. A roster country field is an entity attribute, not an origin declaration.\n\n**What the roster country does not tell you:**\n- Where the harnesses are actually manufactured or assembled. Wire harness production is labour-intensive and heavily offshored; Japanese-domiciled suppliers commonly build in Mexico, the Philippines, Vietnam, Morocco and Eastern Europe, and a US-domiciled supplier like Aptiv builds substantially in Mexico.\n- Whether any USMCA or other preferential treatment applies, which depends on origin rules and content thresholds, not on the supplier's home country.\n- Whether a single supplier sources from multiple plants in different countries for the same part — quite normal, and it would make \"the\" duty rate plural.\n\nSo on this data alone: Yazaki and Sumitomo share a roster country, and Aptiv does not. Whether any of the three face the same duty is not something the roster can settle. You'd need plant-level origin and the relevant HTS classification from each bidder."
          }
        ],
